      body: 'Quantum Key Distribution (QKD) and post-quantum cryptography (PQC) protect data in different ways. QKD, whose first protocol (BB84) was proposed by Charles Bennett and Gilles Brassard in 1984, sends key bits on quantum states of light; eavesdropping disturbs those states and shows up as a higher Quantum Bit Error Rate. PQC is ordinary software on classical computers, built on mathematical problems believed to be hard even for quantum computers; the US National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) released its first three PQC standards, FIPS 203, 204 and 205, in August 2024.',
